clean - takes metadata out of files
===================================

Pictures, PDFs, video and audio carry more than you see: the camera
and its serial number, where a photo was taken, who wrote a document
and with which program, dates, earlier versions, comments. clean takes
it out, in place: the file is replaced by its clean version, and the
original is kept in ~/.system/state/clean first.

    clean FILE...          clean each file
    clean -n FILE...       only say what would go; change nothing
    clean -k FILE...       keep the files' times
    clean -q FILE...       say nothing but errors
    clean -r FILE...       new random bytes, a new hash, even when
                           nothing else goes (before each share)
    clean -u FILE...       undo: put the newest backup back

    find ~/pictures -type f -exec clean -q {} +    a whole directory

It says what it took out:

    photo.jpg: removed EXIF (camera, date, GPS position), thumbnail;
        new random bytes (a hash of its own)
    report.pdf: removed document information (Author, Creator,
        Producer, CreationDate), XMP, file ID, earlier versions and
        unused parts (12)

A file it doesn't know, or one that is broken, is left as it was and
named on stderr (exit status 1), never passed off as clean. Running it
again on a clean file says "nothing to remove".


What goes
---------

JPEG       EXIF (camera, serial number, GPS, dates, program, maker
           notes), its thumbnail (it can show the photo before it was
           cropped), XMP, IPTC/Photoshop, comments, program data, more
           pictures (MPF) and anything after the picture. The rotation
           stays (a tiny EXIF with only that), so photos don't turn
           sideways. The colour profile stays, so colours don't change.
PNG        text (author, program, comments), date, EXIF, pixel size,
           program chunks, anything after the picture
GIF        comments, XMP, program data (the loop setting stays)
WebP       EXIF (rotation kept), XMP
HEIF, AVIF EXIF (camera, GPS, dates) and XMP, blanked where they are
           (the rotation is not in EXIF in these files)
PDF        document information (author, title, program, dates), XMP
           everywhere, program data (PieceInfo: Illustrator keeps the
           whole original there), dates and authors of comments, the
           file ID, and every earlier version that edits left inside
           the file. A new PDF is written from the parts still used;
           pages, fonts and pictures are copied byte for byte.
MP4, MOV,  user data and tags (GPS position, camera, date, program),
M4A        XMP, the creation dates of the file and each track, the
           encoder's name
MKV, WebM  tags, title, date, muxing and writing program, attachments
           (cover pictures, and fonts for subtitles)
MP3        ID3v2 and ID3v1 tags (titles, pictures, comments, program),
           the encoder's name and version in its tag (it becomes
           "LAME", which players need to find the gaps at each end)
Ogg        tags of Vorbis, Opus and Theora (titles, comments, program)
FLAC       tags, pictures, program data
WAV, AVI   INFO tags, ID3, recorder data (bext), XMP
video      the settings text x264, x265 and FFmpeg put into the
           first picture (its bytes become spaces)

Pictures are not decoded and encoded again: they stay exactly the
same. Video and audio can be big, so they are changed where they are:
what goes becomes padding of the same size, so nothing else moves.

The file's times become 2000-01-01 00:00 UTC (-k keeps them).


A hash of its own
-----------------

A file's hash (its SHA-256, say) can follow it: two clean copies of
the same photo would have the same hash, so whoever sees both knows
they are one file, and a hash noted once finds the file again. So each
time clean changes a file it also changes what the format lets vary
without changing the content, at random:

JPEG       0 to 31 fill bytes (0xFF, which decoders skip) before each
           marker
PNG        the picture data split into four chunks at random places
GIF        the picture data in sub-blocks of random sizes
PDF        the four binary bytes on the second line
MP4, MOV,  32 random bytes in a padding box at the end
HEIF, AVIF
Matroska   32 random bytes in a Void element at the end
FLAC       32 random bytes in the padding block
Ogg        32 random bytes in the padding after the tags
WebP       32 random bytes in a chunk of their own at the end (a
           simple WebP gets the extended header that allows one)
MP3        a random "private" bit in the headers of the first 1024
           sound frames (decoders don't read it)
WAV, AVI   32 random bytes in a JUNK chunk at the end

Nothing of it says "clean". clean -r does it again, so each copy you
share gets its own hash. The picture and the sound stay exactly the
same: this was checked by decoding each format before and after.

Not the same thing: "perceptual" hashes (PhotoDNA and the like) look at
the picture itself, and these bytes don't change it. Only changing the
picture (cropping, resizing) changes those.


What it leaves
--------------

- Encrypted PDFs, and signed ones (cleaning breaks the signature, and
  the signature itself names the signer).
- Not yet: TIFF and camera raw files, office documents and EPUB
  (zip), and PDFs packed in ways other than Flate.
- In MP3, the "LAME3.100" LAME writes into unused bits of the sound
  frames: they turned out not to be unused by every decoder, so
  changing them could change the sound.
- What is in the picture or sound itself: faces, places, a voice,
  what a screenshot shows. A screenshot of the whole screen shows
  dwm's bar, with your time.
- Track names and languages in videos, chapters.


The backups
-----------

Each original goes to ~/.system/state/clean (only yours, mode 700),
named by the time (UTC); the file "list" there says which file each
one was. The backups still hold all the metadata: delete them when you
no longer need an undo:

    rm -r ~/.system/state/clean


Build and install
-----------------

Plain C (C89), only libc, static. install.sh builds it into
~/.system/bin (no root needed):

    ~/dotfiles/install.sh clean

By hand (the build makes clean-bin, since "make clean" removes it):

    make
    make PREFIX=$HOME/.system install

Files: clean.c (the command, backups), img.c (JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP),
media.c (video, audio, HEIF), ogg.c (Ogg), pdf.c (PDF), rand.c (the
random bytes),
inflate.c (unpacks the
compressed parts of PDFs, and stops at 64 MB, so a zip bomb can't fill
the memory).
